  {
   "id": "-zFjy6RkAwAJ",
   "from": "Paul Buerkner",
   "author": "6e3dd74b422c061b",
   "date": "2017-09-09T18:02:24Z",
   "subject": "Re: [brms-users] priors for bernoulli model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>The Intercept has an improper flat prior over the reals by default.</div><div><br></div><div>The student_t(3, 0, 10) prior you mention refers to the standard deviation parameter of the s(long, lat) spline.</div><div><br></div><div>Also, you have to keep in mind that all the coefficients are on the logit scale, so it is actually more complicated to reformulate your knowledge about the prevalance into a prior for the Intercept.</div><div><br></div><div>A prevalance of 2% would translate to a value of roughly -3.9 on the logit scale, so maybe one could use this information in the prior by using a normal prior centered around -3.9 for the Intercept.</div><div><br></div><div> However, I don&#39;t think it will change the posterior by much as long as you don&#39;t make the prior too narrow, which you probably don&#39;t want to do anyway.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Paul<br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2017-09-09 19:36 GMT+02:00 Paul Lantos <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">paul....@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br></div></div>"
    ],
