   "subject": "Re: [brms-users] priors for bernoulli model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thanks - I guess a more complicated issue is that the outcome probability varies over space (which is the point of putting in the s(long, lat) term to begin with. So does that suggest a flat prior is the most appropriate for the intercept? Or would I do something like a normal prior over the average of the intercept and keep it loose enough to encompass the high and low extremes of error?</div><div><br></div><div>Most of my data sets are in the 100s to 1000s of observations (this one is particularly large). With say 1000 observations am I still in the territory where the priors wouldn&#39;t make much of an impact?</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Saturday, September 9, 2017 at 2:02:24 PM UTC-4, Paul Buerkner wrote:</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>The Intercept has an improper flat prior over the reals by default.</div><div><br></div><div>The student_t(3, 0, 10) prior you mention refers to the standard deviation parameter of the s(long, lat) spline.</div><div><br></div><div>Also, you have to keep in mind that all the coefficients are on the logit scale, so it is actually more complicated to reformulate your knowledge about the prevalance into a prior for the Intercept.</div><div><br></div><div>A prevalance of 2% would translate to a value of roughly -3.9 on the logit scale, so maybe one could use this information in the prior by using a normal prior centered around -3.9 for the Intercept.</div><div><br></div><div> However, I don&#39;t think it will change the posterior by much as long as you don&#39;t make the prior too narrow, which you probably don&#39;t want to do anyway.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Paul<br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
